### Description

CSV batch segment imports currently materialize the complete upload several times in api/tasks/batch_create_segment_to_index_task.py.
The task:
- loads the entire file with pandas.read_csv();
- iterates it with iterrows(), creating a Series for each row;
- builds a second full list of segment dictionaries;
- sends all content strings in one token-count request;
- retains all DocumentSegment ORM objects until vector indexing begins.

Although uploads have a byte-size limit, there is no row-count limit. A permitted CSV containing many short rows can therefore create millions of objects and substantially amplify worker memory usage.
The import should instead process CSV parsing, token counting, database insertion, and vector indexing in bounded batches while preserving row order, position assignment, word counts, and failure semantics.

### Motivation

Large batch imports can consume several times the uploaded file’s size in memory, spend significant CPU time in iterrows(), and generate an oversized plugin-daemon token-count request. This can block a dataset worker, trigger an out-of-memory restart, or leave an import incomplete.
Bounded processing would:
- keep peak worker memory proportional to the batch size rather than total row count;
- reduce per-row pandas overhead;
- constrain token-count and vector-index requests;
- prevent SQLAlchemy from retaining every imported segment;
- make large but valid CSV imports more predictable and reliable.

### Additional Context

This is distinct from:
- #42232, which addressed the per-row MAX(position) query;
- #38862, which addresses failed imports remaining stuck at waiting;
- #22709, which addressed oversized Celery broker messages.

